  "accuracyFacts": [
    "Operator precedence determines expression grouping; operand evaluation order is specified separately and is generally left to right. Conditional/logical/null-conditional constructs may skip operands or branches.",
    "In C# 14, user-defined compound assignment operators such as operator += can provide in-place behavior; the language fallback uses the corresponding binary operator and assignment.",
    "C# 14 conditional-access assignment skips evaluation of the right-hand expression if its receiver is null; compound assignment is allowed but increment/decrement are not.",
    "Checked/unchecked context applies to specified integral operations and conversions textually inside the context; constant overflow is a compile-time error by default, decimal operations overflow in either context, and unchecked division by zero still throws.",
    "User-defined == and != operators are declared in pairs but their implementations are not required to be logical complements.",
    "C# 15 collection expression with(...) argument element must be followed by a comma before collection elements.",
    "Bitwise and shift operations do not overflow and are unaffected by checked/unchecked context.",
    "C# 14 adds unbound generic type support to nameof; C# 13 allows ^ index-from-end syntax in object initializer indexers; C# 12 introduced collection expressions and C# 15 adds collection expression constructor/factory arguments."
  ]
